// tests/hale/crypto_rsa_pss_test.hl — `std::crypto::rsa_pss_sha256_sign`
// and `rsa_pss_sha256_verify` (GH #1929): RSASSA-PSS over SHA-256, MGF1
// with SHA-256, a 32-byte salt. PSS draws a fresh salt per signature, so
// signing has no fixed vector: a signature made here round-trips through
// verification (two signatures of one message differ and both verify),
// and a signature made by OpenSSL's command line verifies here:
//
//     openssl dgst -sha256 -sigopt rsa_padding_mode:pss \
//         -sigopt rsa_pss_saltlen:32 -sign key.pem msg.txt
//
// over the message "hale rsa-pss fixture message". A signature with
// another salt length, and a PKCS#1 v1.5 one, of the same message by the
// same key do not verify. A downstream handoff signs HTTP requests with
// an RSA key in PSS mode.
//
// THROWAWAY TEST KEYS. Every key in this file was generated for this test
// alone (`openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA`), guards nothing, and is
// published here on purpose. Never use them for anything else.

